.title = "Parser",
.description = "A fast, spec-compliant JavaScript and TypeScript parser written in Zig.",
.author = "Arshad Yaseen",
.date = @date("2026-06-23T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---
Yuku's parser turns JavaScript, TypeScript, and JSX into an [AST](/parser/ast/). It recovers from errors, so a parse with diagnostics still returns a tree of everything it could read.

## [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

```bash
npm install yuku-parser
```

```js
import { parse } from "yuku-parser";

const { program, comments, diagnostics } = parse("const x = 1 + 2;");
```

An [ESTree](https://github.com/estree/estree) / [TypeScript-ESTree](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@typescript-eslint/typescript-estree) AST, matching [Oxc](https://oxc.rs). See [yuku-parser on npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-parser) for the full API.

## [Zig]($section.id('zig'))

```zig
const std = @import("std");
const parser = @import("parser");

pub fn main() !void {
    var tree = try parser.parse(std.heap.smp_allocator, "const x: number = 1;", .{ .lang = .ts });
    defer tree.deinit();

    for (tree.diagnostics.items) |d| {
        std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{d.message});
    }
}
```

`parse` returns a `Tree` holding the AST, its diagnostics, and the source, and fails only with `error.OutOfMemory`. Everything lives in one arena, so `tree.deinit()` frees it all at once. The tree borrows `source`, which must outlive it. [AST](/parser/ast/) shows how to read it.

## [Options]($section.id('options'))

| Field             | Default   | Description                                                                                    |
| ----------------- | --------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `source_type`     | `.module` | `.script`, `.module` (strict, with `import` and `export`), or `.commonjs` (top-level `return`) |
| `lang`            | `.js`     | `.js`, `.ts`, `.jsx`, `.tsx`, or `.dts`                                                        |
| `preserve_parens` | `true`    | Keep `parenthesized_expression` nodes, or only their inner expression                          |
| `comments`        | `.flat`   | `.none`, `.flat`, `.attached`, or `.both`, see [Comments](/parser/ast/#comments)               |
| `tokens`          | `false`   | Keep every token, see [Tokens](/parser/ast/#tokens)                                            |

`.fromPath` infers `source_type` and `lang` from a file name, as in `.lang = .fromPath("app.tsx")`.

## [Diagnostics]($section.id('diagnostics'))

The parser reports a syntax error and parses on past it.

```zig
var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, "const a = 1;\nlet x = ;\nconst y = 2;", .{});
// one diagnostic, "Unexpected token ';'", and a tree holding `a` and `y`
```

Every Yuku tool reports diagnostics in one shape, `ast.Diagnostic`: a `severity` of `.error`, `.warning`, `.hint`, or `.info`, a `message`, a byte `span`, `labels` pointing at related code, and optional `help`.

```zig
for (tree.diagnostics.items) |d| {
    std.debug.print("[{s}] {s} at {d}..{d}\n", .{
        d.severity.toString(), d.message, d.span.start, d.span.end,
    });
    if (d.help) |help| std.debug.print("  help: {s}\n", .{help});
}
```

`tree.hasErrors()` tells whether any error occurred, and `tree.hasDiagnostics()` whether anything was reported. [Semantic analysis](/parser/semantic/) adds the early errors that need scopes, such as duplicate `let` declarations and `break` outside a loop.
